course: world
lesson: 167
title: The rumour that grows
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Monday: "Mr Hughes has a cold." Tuesday: "Mr Hughes is really ill." Wednesday: "Mr Hughes is in hospital." Thursday: Mr Hughes walks in with a cup of tea. Each person told the story a little bigger, and nobody checked the start.
explain: A rumour is a story passed from person to person without anyone checking it. Like the game of Chinese whispers (also called telephone), every telling can change it: words get dropped, details get added, and the boring version gets more exciting.
explain: One mouse in the canteen becomes "rats everywhere, the school's closing". A boy kept in at break becomes "expelled". "Kai's ringtone is annoying" loses one word and becomes "Kai is annoying". A late coach becomes "the coach company went bust".
explain: Fear makes rumours stick. Once kids hear the toilets are haunted, a dripping tap and a creaky door "prove" it. And "everyone knows Leo cheated" sounds like a fact, until you ask, "Who saw it?" and nobody did.
explain: The fix: go back to the start, ask the person who was there, and trust the right source, like the school office. Kids take the mickey out of the runaway story: "Expelled? I'm literally right here eating crisps."
fact: A rumour is a story passed on without being checked; it often grows or changes each time it is told.
fact: Chinese whispers (also called telephone) is a game that shows how a message changes as it passes along a line.
fact: When a story sounds huge, it usually got bigger on the way; the boring first version is usually closest to the truth.
fact: Ask "Who saw it?" and go back to the person who started it.
fact: Losing or changing one small word can change a whole meaning; grown-ups call this taking things out of context.
fact: If someone says "they said this about you", ask the person directly and kindly before getting upset.
fact: For school news the right source is the school office, letter or website, not a rumour on the bus or from another school.
fact: Expulsion, being sent away from a school for good, is very rare and serious.
fact: Rules like "walk, don't run" exist so people don't crash into each other in corridors.
fact: A swallowed cherry stone usually just passes through the body; trees need soil and light, not a tummy.
fact: Old buildings creak, and pipes knock when water moves through them; a boring explanation is more likely than a ghost.
fact: Saying someone cheated without proof is unfair; if nobody saw it, drop it.
fact: Words people use: "Lemsip" is a hot lemon cold drink sold in Britain, "my lovers" is a friendly greeting in the West Country, "bust" means the business ran out of money, "delulu" means believing something silly (young slang), "ya muppets" teases silly people, and "onesies" are all-in-one pyjamas.
exercise: How did "Mr Hughes has a cold" become "Mr Hughes is in hospital"? | Each person told it a little bigger and nobody checked | Mr Hughes really did go into hospital on Wednesday, after his cold got worse | The head teacher announced it in assembly | Mr Hughes told the class himself
exercise: The canteen "rat" story grew into the school closing. What was really there? | A mouse, and pest control came on Friday | Rats everywhere, so the school closed | Nothing at all, it was made up | A squirrel that came in from the playground
exercise: Theo says a Year 6 boy got expelled for throwing a ball. What really happened? | He stayed in at break for one day | He was expelled for a week | He had to change schools | He was banned from football forever
exercise: Someone says the new head hates children because she said "walk, don't run". What is the trap? | Taking one small thing as proof of a big claim | Not listening in assembly | Running in the corridor | Thinking that teachers secretly like children running in the corridors
exercise: Why do schools have "walk, don't run" rules? | So people don't crash into each other | Because head teachers dislike children | Because running wears out the floor | So the corridors stay quiet for lessons
exercise: Josh tells Kai that Mia called him annoying. What did Mia really say? | That Kai's ringtone was annoying | That Kai was the most annoying boy | That Josh was annoying | That she was annoyed with herself
exercise: What is the best thing Kai can do when he hears "Mia said this about you"? | Ask Mia directly and kindly | Tell everyone that Mia is mean | Stop talking to both of them | Spread something about Mia back
exercise: The bus says the trip is cancelled because the coach company went bust. How did it start? | The coach was ten minutes late | The company really went bust | The teacher cancelled the trip | The driver told a joke
exercise: Kids say a cherry stone grows a tree in your tummy. What usually happens to a swallowed cherry stone? | It just passes through your body | It grows a small tree in a month | It stays in your tummy for years | It dissolves straight away
exercise: Priya's non-uniform day message was wrong. Where did it come from? | Her cousin at a different school | The school website | The head teacher's letter | Her class teacher
exercise: What is the right source for school news? | The school office, letter or website | The class group chat on someone's phone | A cousin who goes to a different school | The older kids talking on the school bus
exercise: Why does everyone think the toilets are haunted? | A tap dripped and a door creaked, and fear did the rest | A teacher saw a ghost there | Someone filmed a ghost on a phone | The toilets are much older than the rest of the school, so they must be haunted
exercise: Jade says everyone knows Leo cheated. Who saw it? | Nobody | Jade | The teacher | Half the class
exercise: How did Leo really get a good spelling mark? | He practised every night all week | He copied the words from Jade's test | He saw all the answers early on the desk | He just guessed every single word right
exercise: What do grown-ups call a game where a message changes as it passes along a line? | Chinese whispers, or telephone | Tag | Musical chairs | Hide and seek
test: Mr Hughes: "I hear I was in intensive care? Lovely. I had a sniffle and a Lemsip." Who is he teasing? | The story that grew, not any one child | Himself for being ill | The school nurse | People who drink Lemsip
test: Ella's dad: "So it went from one rat to the school being closed? Sounds like Year 5 just wanted a day off." What is he suggesting? | The rumour grew partly because kids liked the exciting version | The school really should have closed | Year 5 brought the rat in | Rats only come out on school days
test: Year 6 boy: "Expelled? I'm literally right here eating crisps, ya muppets." Why is this the perfect comeback? | He is living proof the rumour is wrong | He is admitting that he really was expelled | He just wants to have everyone else's crisps | He is still angry about the ball game at break
test: Lily: "That's like every head teacher ever. That doesn't mean she's a villain." What is she saying? | One common rule doesn't show how someone feels about children | All head teachers are villains | The new head is the strictest ever | Running is allowed in some schools, so the new head must hate children
test: Kai: "So it was the ringtone, not me? You nearly started World War Three over a ringtone." What went wrong with Josh's message? | One small word was lost and the meaning changed | Mia really did say Kai was annoying | Kai's ringtone is actually nice | Josh made up the whole thing on purpose because he wanted Kai and Mia to fall out
test: Bus driver: "We're not bust, we're stuck behind a tractor, my lovers." What does "my lovers" mean here? | A friendly West Country greeting, like "friends" | That he loves the children personally | That the trip is cancelled | That the tractor is his
test: Dinner lady: "A branch? Out of where, love? I've been here 20 years, never seen a tree walk in." What kind of story is the cherry tree? | A friend-of-a-friend story that kids add bits to | A true story from a science book, about a boy whose tummy grew a tree | A story the dinner lady started | A school rule about fruit
test: What would have happened if Priya had checked the school website first? | She'd have seen it wasn't non-uniform day at her school | She'd have found it was non-uniform day after all | The website would have agreed with her cousin | Half the class would still have come in onesies, whatever the website said
test: Caretaker: "That's not a ghost, kid, that's the pipes. Old building." What is true? | Pipes knock and doors creak in old buildings | The ghost really lives somewhere inside the old pipes | The caretaker is secretly hiding the ghost from them | New buildings are much more haunted than old ones
test: Leo: "I practised every night, my mum's got the flashcards to prove it. You lot are so delulu." What does "delulu" mean? | Believing something silly that isn't true | Very clever | Cheating at tests | Being kind to others
test: A rough voice in the line: "Shut up, the whole school's getting knocked down, my mate's cousin said." What is the best reply? | "Who actually saw it? Let's ask the office." | "Then let's not bother doing our homework any more." | "My cousin says that too, so it must be true, then." | "Quick, go and tell everyone about it before break."
exam: Identify the principal process by which the report "Mr Hughes has a cold" became "Mr Hughes is in hospital" within three days. | Successive retelling without verification, with enlargement at each stage | Formal notification issued by the school office | Medical information released by a hospital | Direct observation by several pupils
exam: Determine the most reliable source of information concerning the arrangements for a school trip. | The school office, letter or website | Conversation among pupils on the bus | A relative attending a different school | A message in a class group chat
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the ingestion of a cherry stone. | It ordinarily passes through the body | It germinates within the stomach | It remains in the body for many years | It produces branches within weeks
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises an accusation of cheating which no person witnessed? | An unsupported claim which ought to be dropped | A fact established by common knowledge | A finding confirmed by the teacher | A conclusion proven by repetition, since many pupils have repeated it
